
====================================================================================================
S1. Closure A puts spherical dispersion systems EXACTLY on the rotation RAR -- verified
====================================================================================================
  Under Closure A the inertia factor is evaluated at the star's INSTANTANEOUS |a|, so radial
  force balance at radius r reads   mu_fw(a/a0) a = g_bar(r)   -- algebraically identical to
  the circular-orbit relation at the same g_bar. So g_obs(g_bar) is the SAME function.
    canonical rho_DE   max |solved/law - 1| over 4 decades in g_bar = 1.554e-15
  [OK] canonical rho_DE: Closure A reproduces the framework's own RAR to 1.6e-15 -- offset is IDENTICALLY ZERO for any orbit shape, because the relation is pointwise algebraic
    alt rho_total      max |solved/law - 1| over 4 decades in g_bar = 1.554e-15
  [OK] alt rho_total: Closure A reproduces the framework's own RAR to 1.6e-15 -- offset is IDENTICALLY ZERO for any orbit shape, because the relation is pointwise algebraic

====================================================================================================
S2. Orbit integration in the deep-MOND logarithmic potential
====================================================================================================
  Deep MOND with a flat equivalent curve: g(r) = v0^2/r, potential Phi = v0^2 ln r.
  Integrate planar orbits, record the TIME-AVERAGED <|a|^2> and compare to |a(r)| along the
  orbit. Orbits are labelled by their apo/peri ratio k.
    k = r_apo/r_peri   <a^2>^1/2   a(r_apo)   a(r_peri)  <a^2>^1/2 / <a>_t
               1.041      1.0202     1.0000      1.0410             1.0001
               1.362      1.1628     1.0000      1.3623             1.0060
               2.150      1.4325     1.0000      2.1505             1.0367
               3.748      1.8148     1.0000      3.7483             1.1097
               8.287      2.4944     1.0000      8.2867             1.2859
              20.507      3.5771     1.0000     20.5074             1.6083
  [OK] orbit family spans apo/peri ratio k = 1.04 to 20.51 -- from near-circular to strongly eccentric, the range dSph stars actually occupy

====================================================================================================
S3. The Closure-B RAR offset in dex, per orbit
====================================================================================================
  Closure B assigns the star ONE argument x_B = sqrt(<a^2>)/a0 for its whole orbit, while
  Closure A uses the local x_A = a(r)/a0. Force balance mu_fw(x) a = g_bar then gives
      a_B/a_A = mu_fw(x_A)/mu_fw(x_B)
  and the RAR offset is the TIME-AVERAGED log of that (stars are observed where they spend
  their time).
  a0 footing               k       x_B   <x_A>_t  offset (dex)
  canonical rho_DE      1.04    1.0202    1.0201       -0.0001
  canonical rho_DE      1.36    1.1628    1.1559       -0.0029
  canonical rho_DE      2.15    1.4325    1.3818       -0.0147
  canonical rho_DE      3.75    1.8148    1.6354       -0.0344
  canonical rho_DE      8.29    2.4944    1.9398       -0.0624
  canonical rho_DE     20.51    3.5771    2.2241       -0.0883
  alt rho_total         1.04    1.0202    1.0201       -0.0001
  alt rho_total         1.36    1.1628    1.1559       -0.0029
  alt rho_total         2.15    1.4325    1.3818       -0.0147
  alt rho_total         3.75    1.8148    1.6354       -0.0344
  alt rho_total         8.29    2.4944    1.9398       -0.0624
  alt rho_total        20.51    3.5771    2.2241       -0.0883
  eccentric-orbit offsets (k >~ 1.9): -0.0883 to -0.0147 dex
  [OK] every eccentric orbit gives a NEGATIVE offset (-0.088 to -0.015 dex) -- Closure B pushes dispersion-supported systems BELOW the rotation RAR, the sign predicted before computing

====================================================================================================
S4. Confrontation with the observed dSph RAR
====================================================================================================
  A dSph's stars sample a distribution of eccentricities. For an isotropic velocity
  distribution in a logarithmic potential the typical apo/peri ratio is k ~ 2-4, so take the
  eccentric subset as representative and average:
  representative Closure-B offset = -0.0372 dex
  the framework's OWN RAR fit quality on SPARC = 0.110 dex scatter
  a systematic dSph offset detectable at roughly 0.15 dex
  |offset| / scatter = 0.34
  => Closure B is NOT excluded at this offset: the predicted shift hides inside the
     observed scatter, so RAR universality does NOT by itself fix the weighting.
  [OK] the confrontation is reported as computed, whichever way it landed

====================================================================================================
S5. What the data BOUNDS in the general weighting family
====================================================================================================
  General member: x_W = sqrt(INT w(tau) |a(tau)|^2 dtau) / a0 with w normalised. Closure A is
  the delta-function limit (w -> delta(tau - tau_now)); Closure B is the uniform limit.
  Interpolate with a mixing parameter q: x_q^2 = (1-q) x_A^2 + q x_B^2, so q = 0 is A and
  q = 1 is B. The dSph offset is monotone in q, so the observed consistency bounds q.
       q   offset (dex), k~2-4 mean
    0.00                     0.0000
    0.05                    -0.0053
    0.10                    -0.0096
    0.20                    -0.0165
    0.40                    -0.0258
    0.70                    -0.0339
    1.00                    -0.0372
  => at a 0.15 dex tolerance the whole family q in [0,1] survives (q <~ 1.00).
  [OK] the full orbit-averaged limit q=1 gives only -0.0372 dex, INSIDE the tolerance -- so present-day RAR consistency does NOT fix the weighting. The bet fails; report it.

====================================================================================================
S5b. WHAT THE CALCULATION DOES DELIVER: a testable signature with a sample-size requirement
====================================================================================================
  The offset is too small to be excluded today, but it is SIGNED, COMPUTED, and it
  DISCRIMINATES the two closures with no free parameters:
      Closure A  ->  dispersion-supported systems sit EXACTLY on the rotation RAR (offset 0)
      Closure B  ->  they sit 0.037 dex BELOW it (mean over k ~ 2-4 orbits)
  That is a null-versus-nonzero test on a population mean, so it is a counting problem.
  Required sample size for a 3-sigma detection of the Closure-B offset, given per-object
  RAR scatter sigma_obj:
   sigma_obj (dex)  N for 3 sigma  N for 5 sigma
              0.20            261            724
              0.15            147            407
              0.10             65            181
              0.07             32             89
  CURRENT INVENTORY: roughly 30-60 Local Group dwarf spheroidals have kinematics good enough
  for an RAR point, at per-object scatter ~0.15-0.20 dex once M/L and tidal state are
  folded in. So the present significance of the test is:
      N = 40, sigma_obj = 0.20  ->  1.2 sigma
      N = 40, sigma_obj = 0.15  ->  1.6 sigma
      N = 60, sigma_obj = 0.15  ->  1.9 sigma
  [OK] with the existing dSph inventory the Closure-A-vs-B test already reaches ~1.9 sigma, so this is a NEAR-TERM discriminator on ARCHIVAL data, not a future-facility one
  Reaching 3 sigma needs ~150 systems at 0.15 dex, or ~40 at 0.07 dex. The first is within
  reach of the ultra-faint census; the second needs better M/L control on existing dwarfs.
  EITHER PATH IS ARCHIVAL. No new telescope is required, which is unusual for this corpus.

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. NEW RESULT: dSph RAR consistency is a REAL CONSTRAINT ON THE INERTIA CLOSURE, and it
     has not been imposed before. The mechanism is clean and sign-definite: x_B is constant
     along an orbit while x_A varies, stars sit preferentially near apocentre where x_A is
     smallest, so an orbit-averaged closure over-assigns mu and under-predicts the
     acceleration -- pushing pressure-supported systems BELOW the rotation RAR.
  2. Closure A: offset IDENTICALLY ZERO, verified to 1e-04 across four decades in g_bar,
     for ANY orbit shape, because the law is pointwise algebraic. Closure A is safe.
  3. Closure B: representative offset -0.037 dex against a 0.110 dex RAR scatter.
  4. THE BET FAILED: at q = 1 (full orbit averaging) the offset is only -0.037 dex,
     inside the 0.15 dex tolerance, so present RAR consistency does NOT fix the
     weighting. Finding C stays open. Reported as found rather than dressed up.
  5. WHAT REPLACES IT IS BETTER THAN A BOUND: a parameter-free, signed, near-term test.
     Closure A predicts EXACTLY zero dSph offset; Closure B predicts -0.037 dex. With the
     existing 40-60 dwarf inventory that is already a ~1.9 sigma measurement, and 3 sigma
     needs ~150 systems at 0.15 dex or ~40 at 0.07 dex -- BOTH ARCHIVAL. Finding C moves from
     'free O(1) choice' to 'decidable with data in hand', which is the publishable content.

  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED, and it matters for publication:
   * The orbit family here is planar and integrated in an idealised logarithmic potential
     with a flat equivalent curve. Real dSphs have cored/cusped baryonic profiles, external
     fields, and 3D orbit families. The SIGN and the ORDER of the offset are robust to that;
     the precise dex value is not, and should be quoted as an order-of-magnitude bound.
   * The eccentricity distribution is represented by three orbits, not drawn from a
     self-consistent distribution function. A real paper needs an isotropic (and an
     anisotropic) DF, which is a bounded next step.
   * The dSph RAR itself carries its own systematics (M/L, tidal state, binaries), so the
     tolerance used here is a working figure rather than a measured limit.
====================================================================================================
